---
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# rule.mdc.example
# Template for a rule. Rules are always-on (or pattern-matched) directives
# that constrain output — e.g. "no bare TODOs", "prefer typed errors".
#
# Rules are FLAT files at rules/<name>.mdc. The .mdc extension matches
# Cursor's convention; the toolkit transforms the frontmatter at install
# time so each tool gets the right shape.
#
# Copy to rules/<your-rule-name>.mdc, edit, then run `make register`.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

name: example-rule
description: One-line description of what this rule enforces.
author: your-name

presets:
  - quality-gates

# Allowlist. Rules only really land in tools that have a "rule"
# concept — Cursor has rules/, Claude Code has a speculative
# .claude/rules/ (not auto-loaded, see verification-matrix.md).
tools:
  - cursor
  - claude-code

# Per-tool frontmatter overrides. The tool config in
# config/tools.json declares the default frontmatter shape for each
# tool's rule destination; these overrides win.
overrides:
  cursor:
    # Cursor rule frontmatter:
    #   description: "{description}"   ← filled from above
    #   globs: "**/*"                  ← which files this rule applies to
    #   alwaysApply: true              ← attached to every prompt, no glob match needed
    alwaysApply: true
    globs: "**/*.{ts,tsx,js,jsx,py,go,rs,java,kt,rb}"
  # claude-code:
  #   (Claude Code doesn't have a structured rule frontmatter contract;
  #   the body lands at .claude/rules/<name>.md and must be referenced
  #   from CLAUDE.md to actually load. See docs/verification-matrix.md.)
---

# Rule: example rule

Open with a one-line statement of what the rule requires.

## Good

```
// example of compliant code
```

## Bad

```
// example of code the rule flags
```

## Rationale

Why does this rule exist? "Bare TODOs rot." "Generic Error types
defeat structured error handling." Tie the rule to a concrete failure
mode it prevents.

## When this rule does NOT apply

Be honest. Most rules have exceptions; rules that pretend they don't
get worked around.

## Gotchas

- **`alwaysApply: true` vs `globs` matters in Cursor.** With
  `alwaysApply: true`, the rule attaches to every prompt regardless
  of which files are open. With `alwaysApply: false` plus a `globs`
  pattern, the rule only fires when files matching the pattern are
  in context. Most rules want `alwaysApply: false` plus a glob — be
  intentional if you turn `alwaysApply` on.
- **`globs` is a comma-separated string OR a string with brace
  expansion, depending on Cursor version.** The safe shape is a
  single string like `"**/*.{ts,js}"`. Multi-glob arrays are not
  universally supported.
- **Claude Code does not auto-load `.claude/rules/`.** The toolkit
  drops files there for parking; you wire them up by `@`-importing
  them from `CLAUDE.md` (or another always-loaded context file).
- **Rules vs skills.** A rule constrains output ("don't do X").
  A skill provides knowledge ("here's how to think about X"). If
  the asset's verb is "should", it's probably a rule; if it's
  "consider", it's probably a skill. Same source content can serve
  both purposes via per-tool mapping (Cursor maps skills→rules, so
  a skill body becomes a rule on disk for Cursor).

